module GameData
  module UnitHistories
    HISTORIES = {
      "aedui" => "The Aedui were one of central Gaul's most powerful peoples and long styled themselves friends and allies of Rome. Their political divisions—especially the rivalry surrounding Dumnorix—made that alliance a recurring problem for Caesar.",
      "allobroges" => "The Allobroges occupied the Rhône valley and approaches to the Alps. Rome had conquered them before the Gallic War, making their territory an important link between Transalpine Gaul and Caesar's campaigns farther north.",
      "andes" => "The Andes, also called the Andecavi, lived around the lower Loire near modern Angers. They joined the western rising against Rome and later contributed forces to the great relief army sent toward Alesia.",
      "arverni" => "The Arverni were a wealthy and influential people of the central highlands. Once dominant across much of Gaul, they supplied the coalition of 52 BC with its most famous leader, Vercingetorix.",
      "arvii" => "The Arvii represent one of the smaller communities associated with the Bituriges region in the game. The surviving ancient record preserves far less about them than it does about their more powerful neighbors.",
      "atrebates" => "The Atrebates were a Belgic people centered near modern Arras. Their king Commius first aided Caesar, including in Britain, before becoming one of Rome's persistent opponents late in the war.",
      "atuatuci" => "The Atuatuci were a Belgic people who claimed descent from warriors left behind by the Cimbri and Teutones. Caesar besieged their stronghold in 57 BC after they came too late to join the Nervii in battle.",
      "belgae" => "The Belgae were the northern peoples of Gaul, linked by culture and repeated military coalitions rather than a single state. Caesar regarded their distance from Roman influence and frequent warfare as sources of their formidable reputation.",
      "bellovaci" => "The Bellovaci, centered around modern Beauvais, were among the strongest Belgic peoples. They fielded major forces in 57 BC and mounted another determined resistance during Caesar's final campaigns in 51 BC.",
      "bituriges" => "The Bituriges Cubi occupied the country around Avaricum, modern Bourges. They joined Vercingetorix in 52 BC, but persuaded him to spare Avaricum from the scorched-earth policy; Caesar subsequently captured the city after a famous siege.",
      "boii" => "The Boii were a far-ranging Celtic people with communities across central Europe. Survivors who accompanied the Helvetii in 58 BC were settled among the Aedui after Caesar defeated the migration.",
      "cadurci" => "The Cadurci lived in the Quercy region of southwestern Gaul. Their leader Lucterius continued resistance after Alesia, and the siege of Uxellodunum in Cadurcan country became one of the war's final episodes.",
      "caletes" => "The Caletes occupied the Pays de Caux in coastal Normandy. They joined the Belgic coalition of 57 BC and later took part in the maritime and western resistance to Roman rule.",
      "cantiaci" => "The Cantiaci inhabited much of modern Kent. Caesar encountered several of their kings during his second British expedition in 54 BC, when the southeastern tribes alternated between resistance and negotiated submission.",
      "carnutes" => "The Carnutes occupied the country around Cenabum, modern Orléans, and their territory held religious importance for the Gauls. Their killing of Roman merchants at Cenabum helped ignite the general revolt of 52 BC.",
      "cenomani" => "The Aulerci Cenomani lived around modern Le Mans. They belonged to the network of western Gallic peoples that supplied men to the wider coalitions raised against Caesar.",
      "eburones" => "The Eburones lived between the Meuse and Rhine under Ambiorix and Catuvolcus. In 54 BC they destroyed a Roman column of roughly fifteen cohorts, provoking Caesar's exceptionally harsh retaliatory campaigns.",
      "elusates" => "The Elusates were an Aquitanian people of the southwest. They joined the resistance to Publius Crassus in 56 BC and submitted after the Roman campaign broke the Aquitanian coalition.",
      "esuvii" => "The Esuvii were a people of northwestern Gaul, probably in Normandy. They first submitted to Rome, then joined neighboring coastal peoples in renewing resistance during the campaign of 56 BC.",
      "helvetii" => "The Helvetii lived on the Swiss plateau. Their attempted migration into western Gaul in 58 BC brought Caesar into open war and ended with their defeat near Bibracte and forced return home.",
      "helvii" => "The Helvii lived along the northern edge of the Roman province and remained Roman allies. During the revolt of 52 BC they fought neighboring peoples aligned with Vercingetorix.",
      "lemovicii" => "The Lemovicii occupied the Limousin region. They joined the coalition of 52 BC; their chief Sedullos was killed while leading his contingent in the great battle around Alesia.",
      "leuci" => "The Leuci occupied the upper Moselle around modern Toul. They generally cooperated with Caesar and were among the Gallic communities that supplied grain to the Roman army.",
      "luxovii" => "The Luxovii—usually called the Lexovii in ancient sources—lived in Normandy. They rebelled during the western campaign of 56 BC and joined a coalition led by Viridovix.",
      "mandubii" => "The Mandubii were the small people whose hilltop stronghold was Alesia. Their homeland became the scene of the decisive siege of 52 BC, and civilians expelled from the fortress suffered between the opposing armies.",
      "mediomatrici" => "The Mediomatrici lived around Divodurum, modern Metz, between the Moselle and the Rhine frontier. Their territory lay on one of the principal approaches between Gaul and Germania.",
      "menapi" => "The Menapi occupied marshy and wooded country near the lower Rhine and Meuse. They used terrain and dispersal to resist set-piece battle and were among the last northern peoples Caesar compelled to submit.",
      "morini" => "The Morini lived along the Channel coast opposite Britain. Their wetlands and forests frustrated Roman operations, while their ports made their territory important to Caesar's British expeditions.",
      "namnetes" => "The Namnetes lived near the Loire estuary around modern Nantes. They joined the Veneti and other Atlantic peoples in the maritime rising of 56 BC.",
      "nantuates" => "The Nantuates occupied the upper Rhône valley near the Alpine passes. Caesar's legate Servius Galba campaigned against them in 57 BC while trying to secure the route through the Great St Bernard region.",
      "nervii" => "The Nervii were a powerful Belgic people famed for the surprise attack on Caesar's army at the Sabis in 57 BC. They came close to breaking the Roman force before the battle turned against them.",
      "osismi" => "The Osismi lived at the western end of Brittany. Their coastal position tied them to the Veneti-led maritime coalition that resisted Rome in 56 BC.",
      "pictones" => "The Pictones occupied Poitou along the Atlantic approaches. Their ships and ports gave them a role in Caesar's western campaigns, while later divisions drew parts of the community into the struggle of 52 BC.",
      "remi" => "The Remi were a leading Belgic people centered on Durocortorum, modern Reims. They allied with Caesar at the beginning of the Belgic campaign and supplied him with intelligence, provisions, and political support.",
      "ruteni" => "The Ruteni lived around modern Rodez. Part of the people lay within Rome's province, while the independent Ruteni joined the Arvernian-led revolt of 52 BC.",
      "santones" => "The Santones occupied the Atlantic country around modern Saintes. The Helvetii reportedly intended to migrate toward their lands, and they later contributed to the broad Gallic coalition against Caesar.",
      "senones" => "The Senones lived around modern Sens. Initially cooperative, they later resisted Roman demands; Caesar executed their leader Acco after suppressing the rising of 53 BC.",
      "sequani" => "The Sequani controlled the upper Saône and approaches to the Jura. They invited Ariovistus across the Rhine against their Aedui rivals, only to find themselves dominated by the German king before Caesar intervened.",
      "sotiates" => "The Sotiates were an Aquitanian people encountered by Publius Crassus in 56 BC. Their leader Adiatunnus defended a fortified town and attempted a dramatic sortie before surrendering.",
      "tarbelli" => "The Tarbelli lived in southwestern Aquitania near modern Dax. They were among the peoples brought to terms during Publius Crassus's Aquitanian campaign of 56 BC.",
      "tolosates" => "The Tolosates represent the peoples around Tolosa, modern Toulouse, on the borderlands of the Roman province. The region was closely associated with the Volcae Tectosages and the routes linking Aquitania to the Mediterranean.",
      "treveri" => "The Treveri occupied the Moselle valley and were renowned for cavalry. Under leaders such as Indutiomarus they repeatedly challenged Roman authority and maintained close connections across the Rhine.",
      "venelli" => "The Venelli, called the Unelli in many ancient texts, inhabited the Cotentin peninsula. Under Viridovix they led a large western coalition against Caesar's legate Sabinus in 56 BC.",
      "veneti" => "The Veneti were the foremost maritime power of southern Brittany. Their high-sided ships and fortified coastal sites forced Caesar to build a fleet for the decisive naval campaign of 56 BC.",
      "volcae" => "The Volcae were a broad Celtic grouping of southern Gaul; the Tectosages were centered around Tolosa. Their proximity to the Roman province made their lands strategically important throughout the Gallic War.",
      "ariovistus" => "Ariovistus was a German king invited into Gaul by the Sequani during their war with the Aedui. After establishing his own power west of the Rhine, he was defeated by Caesar in 58 BC and driven back toward Germania.",
      "german_marcomanni" => "The Marcomanni were a Germanic people whose name is often interpreted as 'men of the frontier.' Caesar lists them among the peoples serving in Ariovistus's army in 58 BC.",
      "german_tencteri" => "The Tencteri were a Germanic people especially associated with horsemanship. Together with the Usipetes they crossed the Rhine in 55 BC and were violently defeated during negotiations with Caesar.",
      "german_usipetes" => "The Usipetes crossed the Rhine with the Tencteri in 55 BC after pressure from neighboring peoples. Caesar's attack scattered their migration and led directly to his first bridge crossing into Germania.",
      "ambiorix" => "Ambiorix was a king of the Eburones and architect of the Roman disaster of 54 BC. He escaped Caesar's repeated efforts to capture him and became the most elusive Belgic leader of the war.",
      "dumnorix" => "Dumnorix was a wealthy Aedui noble, cavalry leader, and political rival to Rome's supporters within his tribe. Caesar had him killed in 54 BC after he tried to leave the army before the second expedition to Britain.",
      "vercingetorix" => "Vercingetorix united a broad Gallic coalition in 52 BC and adopted a strategy of scorched earth and concentrated resistance. After defeat at Alesia he surrendered to Caesar and was later executed in Rome.",
      "legion_i" => "Pompey loaned the First Legion to Caesar after the destruction of a Roman legion in the Eburones disaster. It represents the reinforcements Rome made available as Caesar's army expanded late in the war.",
      "legion_v" => "The Fifth Legion represents one of the new formations raised when the revolt of Vercingetorix forced Caesar to enlarge his army in 52 BC. It later became famous under the title Alaudae.",
      "legion_vi" => "The Sixth Legion was raised during the emergency of 52 BC as Caesar mobilized against Vercingetorix. The later Sixth Ferrata traced a celebrated tradition through the civil wars that followed.",
      "legion_vii" => "The Seventh was one of Caesar's four veteran legions at the opening of the Gallic War. It served throughout the conquest and belonged to the experienced core on which his early campaigns depended.",
      "legion_viii" => "The Eighth was one of Caesar's established veteran legions before 58 BC. It fought through the Gallic campaigns and later followed him into the civil war.",
      "legion_ix" => "The Ninth was among Caesar's original veteran legions in Gaul. Its long service helped form the professional nucleus of the army that campaigned from the Alps to the Channel.",
      "legion_x" => "The Tenth—Caesar's celebrated Legio X Equestris—was his favored veteran formation. Its reliability became legendary after Caesar used it as mounted infantry during the tense negotiations with Ariovistus.",
      "legion_xi" => "Caesar raised the Eleventh before launching the campaign of 58 BC, expanding beyond the four legions already stationed in the province. It received its first hard campaigning against the Helvetii and Ariovistus.",
      "legion_xii" => "The Twelfth was raised alongside the Eleventh at the start of the Gallic War. It fought at the Sabis and remained part of Caesar's growing army throughout the conquest.",
      "legion_xiii" => "The Thirteenth was recruited after Caesar's first campaigning seasons as the scale of the war widened. It later became closely associated with Caesar and famously crossed the Rubicon with him.",
      "legion_xiv" => "The Fourteenth was raised as part of Caesar's early reinforcement of the Gallic army. Much of it was destroyed in the Eburones ambush of 54 BC, after which Caesar rebuilt or replaced the formation.",
      "legion_xv" => "The Fifteenth represents the later growth of Caesar's field army as revolts and garrison demands multiplied. The surviving sources make the identity and continuity of some late-war legion numbers less certain than those of Caesar's veteran core."
    }.freeze

    module_function

    def fetch(unit_type)
      HISTORIES.fetch(unit_type.key) do
        "#{unit_type.name} is represented as a #{unit_type.category} formation in Caesar's Gallic War. The surviving ancient record provides only limited detail about this particular contingent."
      end
    end
  end
end
